---
/*  What the decoder adds to the window every plugin shares: its outputs in the header, the headphones' and the
    speakers' pages, the loudspeaker table, the orientation strip where the effects have their matrix, and its
    scene. The pictures are the decoder's own, drawn by its picture tool. */
import TutorialPage from '../../layouts/TutorialPage.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const img = (name: string) => `${base}/tutorial/${name}.webp`;
const window = `${base}/tutorial/the-window`;

const parts: [string, string][] = [
  ['outputs', 'outputs'],
  ['headphones', 'headphones'],
  ['speakers', 'speakers'],
  ['loudspeakers', 'loudspeakers'],
  ['orientation', 'orientation'],
  ['scene', 'the scene'],
  ['try', 'things to try'],
];
---

<TutorialPage
  id="decoder"
  sub="the field out to loudspeakers or headphones, turned with the listener's head"
  parts={parts}
  window={{ src: img('decoder-window'), alt: 'A decoder window: headphones and speakers in the header, the scene with the head and the loudspeakers, the orientation strip, and the headphones page.', caption: 'a decoder window' }}
>
  <div class="prose" slot="intro">
    <p class="lede">
      The decoder is where the field leaves bambi: to headphones, to loudspeakers, or to both at once, turned with
      the listener's head.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its window is the one every bambi plugin has, with more of it its own than in any other plugin: outputs in
      the header, and where the others have their matrix, the head. The scene, presets and the link are explained
      in <a href={window}>the window</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="outputs">
    <h2>outputs</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('decoder-outputs')} alt="The header's outputs: headphones open, speakers beside it, each with its box." width="640" height="88" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Where the other plugins have their instances, the decoder's header has its two outputs,
        <code>headphones</code> and <code>speakers</code>; with another decoder in the session, a switch beside them
        shows and edits that one. <strong>The box</strong> switches an output on; <strong>the name</strong> opens its
        page in the panel. Both can play at once, each on channels of its own. If both are given the same channels,
        the page says so, and says what playing would do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="headphones" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-headphones')} alt="The headphones page: model, ku100 or sofa file; head and ears; the channel pair; through the layout; personalise." width="696" height="720" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>headphones</h2>
      <p>The head the field is heard through:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>model</code></strong> is learned from measured heads, and two sliders make it yours:
          <code>head</code>, 0.75 to 1.3 ×, scales the time and the low level difference between the ears, and
          <code>ears</code>, ±2 erb, moves the ears' notches.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>ku100</code></strong> is a dummy head as measured, and <strong><code>sofa file</code></strong>
          loads any measured head. On a measured head the ears stay as they are, and <code>head</code> scales only
          the time difference.
        </li>
        <li><strong>channels</strong>: the pair of the host's channels the headphones play on.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>through the layout</code></strong> plays the speakers page's layout on the headphones, so you
          hear the loudspeakers without them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>personalise…</code></strong> opens the page for finding your sliders by ear, against a real
          loudspeaker.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="speakers" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-speakers')} alt="The speakers page: itu, file or typed; ten layouts with 4+7+0 chosen; from channel; the channel map; one listener or audience; distance compensation; loudspeakers." width="696" height="860" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>speakers</h2>
      <p>What the loudspeakers are, in three ways:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>itu</code></strong>: ten layouts built in, from <code>0+2+0</code> to <code>9+10+3</code>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>file</code></strong>: an IEM AllRADecoder or AmbDec layout, decoded by bambi, or a whole
          decoder as SOFA filters, played as it is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>typed</code></strong>: the <a href="#loudspeakers">table</a> is the layout. A loaded one
          becomes typed the moment you edit it there; a new one starts empty.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>channels</strong>: <code>from channel</code> is where the layout starts on the host's channels; its
        channels move as a block, so a headphone pair or an interface's own outputs can sit before them. Under it,
        every channel is marked with the output that writes it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>options</strong>: whether the field is weighted for <code>one listener</code> or an
        <code>audience</code>, and <code>distance compensation</code>, which gives every loudspeaker the gain and
        delay that bring it to the farthest one's distance. A layout of two loudspeakers has its own choice instead of the weighting:
        <code>fold</code> puts the whole field on the pair, and <code>slice</code> plays only what the head points
        at, between them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="loudspeakers" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-loudspeakers')} alt="The loudspeakers table: a row each with its azimuth, elevation, distance, gain, delay and a test button." width="696" height="820" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>loudspeakers</h2>
      <p><code>loudspeakers…</code> opens the table, one loudspeaker to a row:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Drag a number</strong> as you drag a tile: its channel, azimuth, elevation and distance. Gain and
          delay are what distance compensation gives it, and are read only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The box at the left</strong> makes a loudspeaker imaginary: it shapes the layout but is never fed.
        </li>
        <li><strong><code>test</code></strong> sends a burst on that loudspeaker's channel alone, so you can check each by ear.</li>
        <li><strong>The cross</strong> removes the row.</li>
        <li><strong><code>add a loudspeaker</code></strong> adds one ahead, at the farthest distance, on the next free channel.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="orientation">
    <h2>orientation</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('decoder-orientation')} alt="The orientation strip: none, osc, airpods and phone; head or scene, recentre, setup and static; yaw, pitch and roll with their rates." width="1236" height="420" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Where the effects have their matrix, the decoder has the head, always on screen. The sliders and rates turn
        the scene for every output; a tracker set to <code>head</code> turns the headphones and a sliced pair.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What tracks the head</strong>: <code>none</code>, <code>osc</code>, <code>airpods</code> or a
          <code>phone</code>. AirPods and a phone go through a helper that comes with the decoder, on macOS. Beside
          the tabs, the tracker says whether it is speaking, and how often.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>head</code></strong> or <strong><code>scene</code></strong>: whether the tracker's turn is
          undone as a head, so the sound stays put in the room while you turn, or turns the scene one more time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>recentre</code></strong> makes the way you face now the front, and <strong><code>setup…</code></strong>
          opens the tracker's own page: the OSC port and message, the AirPods' look-and-nod, or the code a phone
          scans.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>yaw</code></strong>, <strong><code>pitch</code></strong> and <strong><code>roll</code></strong>
          turn the scene by hand, each with a rate under it. <code>flip</code> beside an angle turns the tracker's
          sign on that axis around.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>static</code></strong> holds the scene still whatever the sliders and rates say, to hear a
          mix stand still while its turn is automated.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scene">
    <h2>the scene</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('decoder-scene')} alt="The scene on the speakers page: the head at the centre, the loudspeakers numbered, and the hull between them." width="1236" height="602" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The decoder draws the head at the centre — on the globe with its eyes either side of where it looks, on the
        equirect as a cross at the gaze — and the loudspeakers around it, numbered. While the speakers page is
        open, the hull between them is drawn too. An output that is not playing is drawn faint, and an imaginary
        loudspeaker dashed. The energy is what every output hears. Two switches are the decoder's own:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>head frame</code></strong> draws the scene as the head sees it: the equirect's front becomes the gaze.</li>
        <li><strong><code>sources</code></strong> shows where the session's sources are heard, turned with the scene.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>things to try</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>A stereo pair that follows your head.</strong> On the speakers page choose <code>0+2+0</code> and
          <code>slice</code>, then a tracker set to <code>head</code>: the pair plays only what you face.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Loudspeakers on headphones.</strong> Choose a layout on the speakers page, then switch on
          <code>through the layout</code> on the headphones page to hear it before you set it up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turn the room, not the head.</strong> Give <code>yaw rate</code> a value and press play: the whole
          scene turns round you, and every output hears it.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</TutorialPage>
